WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.470
In 2027, that alone probably won't pay the bills the way it does today.

2
00:00:04.893 --> 00:00:06.133
If you work in AI or software

3
00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:08.513
development and make a living building automations

4
00:00:08.513 --> 00:00:10.133
and agents, I have some news.

5
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:13.153
In 2027, that alone probably won't pay

6
00:00:13.153 --> 00:00:14.633
the bills the way it does today.

7
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:16.273
Not because the work will disappear.

8
00:00:16.273 --> 00:00:17.913
But because agents will do a lot

9
00:00:17.913 --> 00:00:18.273
of it.

10
00:00:18.273 --> 00:00:20.153
And your client will have access to

11
00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:20.933
the same agent.

12
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:21.973
So in this video,

13
00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:23.333
I want to talk about what I

14
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:24.633
think you need to learn now to

15
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:25.233
keep earning.

16
00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:26.553
And it's not another tool.

17
00:00:26.553 --> 00:00:27.753
Think about your work today.

18
00:00:27.753 --> 00:00:28.713
Getting the brief,

19
00:00:28.713 --> 00:00:29.873
connecting the API,

20
00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:30.993
building the workflow,

21
00:00:30.993 --> 00:00:32.053
tuning the prompt,

22
00:00:32.053 --> 00:00:32.793
delivering.

23
00:00:32.793 --> 00:00:34.253
The agent already does a lot of

24
00:00:34.253 --> 00:00:34.433
that.

25
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:35.893
And it gets better with every new

26
00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:36.893
generation of models.

27
00:00:36.893 --> 00:00:37.973
When anyone can build,

28
00:00:37.973 --> 00:00:39.273
building becomes a commodity.

29
00:00:39.273 --> 00:00:41.133
And commodities compete on price.

30
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.713
If your pitch is “I'll build your

31
00:00:42.713 --> 00:00:44.493
agent,” you're bidding against the price of

32
00:00:44.493 --> 00:00:45.273
a subscription.

33
00:00:45.273 --> 00:00:46.953
But there's one thing clients can't figure

34
00:00:46.953 --> 00:00:47.733
out on their own,

35
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:49.213
even with the best agent in the

36
00:00:49.213 --> 00:00:49.493
world.

37
00:00:49.493 --> 00:00:50.253
Knowing what to do.

38
00:00:50.253 --> 00:00:51.533
Which process to tackle first.

39
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:53.213
What's worth automating and what isn't.

40
00:00:53.213 --> 00:00:54.153
Where the risks are.

41
00:00:54.153 --> 00:00:55.853
And whether what was built is actually

42
00:00:55.853 --> 00:00:56.333
working.

43
00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:57.653
That's where the money will be in

44
00:00:57.653 --> 00:00:58.433
2027.

45
00:00:58.433 --> 00:00:59.673
And I see four roles.

46
00:00:59.673 --> 00:01:00.973
The first is the consultant.

47
00:01:00.973 --> 00:01:02.453
Instead of starting with the tool,

48
00:01:02.453 --> 00:01:03.793
you start with the client's process.

49
00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:04.613
You go in,

50
00:01:04.613 --> 00:01:05.393
map it out,

51
00:01:05.393 --> 00:01:06.633
and understand what comes in,

52
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:08.213
who decides, who does the work,

53
00:01:08.213 --> 00:01:09.533
and where time gets wasted.

54
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:11.153
Then you deliver an assessment and a

55
00:01:11.153 --> 00:01:12.833
plan: this stays with a person,

56
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:13.813
this goes to an agent,

57
00:01:13.813 --> 00:01:15.433
this remains traditional automation,

58
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:16.893
and this should stop existing.

59
00:01:16.893 --> 00:01:18.493
This is the hardest part to automate,

60
00:01:18.493 --> 00:01:20.233
because it takes understanding the business,

61
00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:21.493
not just the technology.

62
00:01:21.493 --> 00:01:23.253
The second is supporting the client over

63
00:01:23.253 --> 00:01:23.733
time.

64
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:25.333
An AI project doesn't end when you

65
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:26.013
deliver it.

66
00:01:26.013 --> 00:01:27.133
The model changes,

67
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:27.873
the agent changes,

68
00:01:27.873 --> 00:01:28.533
the process changes.

69
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:30.333
If you support the client month after

70
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:31.673
month, adjust the context,

71
00:01:31.673 --> 00:01:32.333
review the rules,

72
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:33.353
and measure results,

73
00:01:33.353 --> 00:01:34.313
you become a partner.

74
00:01:34.313 --> 00:01:35.973
And partners get recurring contracts.

75
00:01:35.973 --> 00:01:36.913
A builder's project ends.

76
00:01:36.913 --> 00:01:37.993
The third is the mentor.

77
00:01:37.993 --> 00:01:39.453
Your client will want to use agents

78
00:01:39.453 --> 00:01:40.033
themselves.

79
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:40.833
You can fight that,

80
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:42.013
or you can teach them.

81
00:01:42.013 --> 00:01:43.693
A mentor teaches managers and teams to

82
00:01:43.693 --> 00:01:44.433
work with agents:

83
00:01:44.433 --> 00:01:46.213
provide context, delegate,

84
00:01:46.213 --> 00:01:48.153
set limits, and evaluate.

85
00:01:48.153 --> 00:01:49.473
Instead of doing it for them,

86
00:01:49.473 --> 00:01:51.113
you help them get good at it.

87
00:01:51.113 --> 00:01:52.133
And when you teach,

88
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:53.593
you become the go-to person at the

89
00:01:53.593 --> 00:01:54.033
company.

90
00:01:54.033 --> 00:01:55.673
And the fourth is a role almost

91
00:01:55.673 --> 00:01:56.993
no one has taken on yet:

92
00:01:56.993 --> 00:01:57.433
auditor.

93
00:01:57.433 --> 00:01:59.693
When companies have dozens of agents running,

94
00:01:59.693 --> 00:02:00.973
someone needs to take a look.

95
00:02:00.973 --> 00:02:02.273
What can this agent do?

96
00:02:02.273 --> 00:02:03.893
Does it have too much autonomy?

97
00:02:03.893 --> 00:02:05.753
Is it using data it shouldn't?

98
00:02:05.753 --> 00:02:07.233
Who approved this decision?

99
00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:08.313
Are the results good,

100
00:02:08.313 --> 00:02:10.293
or do they just look good?

101
00:02:10.293 --> 00:02:12.013
Auditing agents means reviewing process,

102
00:02:12.013 --> 00:02:13.293
risk, and results.

103
00:02:13.293 --> 00:02:15.133
And your technical background gives you an

104
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:16.813
edge here: you know where agents tend

105
00:02:16.813 --> 00:02:17.393
to go wrong.

106
00:02:17.393 --> 00:02:19.033
So, what should you learn now?

107
00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:20.353
First: processes.

108
00:02:20.353 --> 00:02:21.293
How to map,

109
00:02:21.293 --> 00:02:23.173
measure, and redesign a process.

110
00:02:23.173 --> 00:02:24.813
Second: agent management.

111
00:02:24.813 --> 00:02:26.253
Goals, context, tools,

112
00:02:26.253 --> 00:02:28.253
autonomy, evaluation, and oversight.

113
00:02:28.253 --> 00:02:29.793
Third: the client's business.

114
00:02:29.793 --> 00:02:32.173
Pick an industry and understand it deeply.

115
00:02:32.173 --> 00:02:33.713
Fourth: communication.

116
00:02:33.713 --> 00:02:34.753
Learn to explain,

117
00:02:34.753 --> 00:02:36.993
teach, and persuade a manager who's not

118
00:02:36.993 --> 00:02:37.513
technical.

119
00:02:37.513 --> 00:02:39.253
And fifth: evaluation.

120
00:02:39.253 --> 00:02:40.153
Learn to judge,

121
00:02:40.153 --> 00:02:41.133
with clear criteria,

122
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:43.353
whether an agent is delivering results.

123
00:02:43.353 --> 00:02:44.813
Notice that none of these is a

124
00:02:44.813 --> 00:02:45.213
tool.

125
00:02:45.213 --> 00:02:47.433
And no, your technical background isn't losing

126
00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:47.933
its value.

127
00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:49.093
Its role is changing.

128
00:02:49.093 --> 00:02:50.233
Before, it was the product.

129
00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:51.893
Now, it's what gives you the credibility

130
00:02:51.893 --> 00:02:53.253
to diagnose, advise,

131
00:02:53.253 --> 00:02:54.053
and audit.

132
00:02:54.053 --> 00:02:55.273
You don't have to be the best

133
00:02:55.273 --> 00:02:55.953
builder anymore.

134
00:02:55.953 --> 00:02:57.593
You need to understand the technology well

135
00:02:57.593 --> 00:02:59.413
enough to make good decisions and explain

136
00:02:59.413 --> 00:03:00.133
them clearly.

137
00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:01.353
How can you start this week?

138
00:03:01.353 --> 00:03:01.953
Pick a client,

139
00:03:01.953 --> 00:03:03.033
or your own company.

140
00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.253
Choose a process.

141
00:03:04.253 --> 00:03:06.073
Assess it before building anything:

142
00:03:06.073 --> 00:03:07.033
what comes in,

143
00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:08.993
who decides, where the bottleneck is,

144
00:03:08.993 --> 00:03:10.473
and what should go to a person,

145
00:03:10.473 --> 00:03:12.173
an agent, or automation.

146
00:03:12.173 --> 00:03:13.773
Write it all on one page.

147
00:03:13.773 --> 00:03:16.013
That document is already a consulting product.

148
00:03:16.013 --> 00:03:17.253
At INEMA dot club,

149
00:03:17.253 --> 00:03:19.113
there's a course on Agent Management with

150
00:03:19.113 --> 00:03:20.233
seven principles,

151
00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:21.713
and an area on AI Management in

152
00:03:21.713 --> 00:03:22.053
events.

153
00:03:22.053 --> 00:03:22.773
It's all open.

154
00:03:22.773 --> 00:03:24.193
Use it as a starting point.

155
00:03:24.193 --> 00:03:26.433
In 2027, the client will have the

156
00:03:26.433 --> 00:03:27.293
same agent you do.

157
00:03:27.293 --> 00:03:28.753
The difference will be knowing what to

158
00:03:28.753 --> 00:03:29.273
do with it.

159
00:03:29.273 --> 00:03:30.253
Stop selling builds.

160
00:03:30.253 --> 00:03:31.293
Start selling clarity,

161
00:03:31.293 --> 00:03:32.673
ongoing support, teaching,

162
00:03:32.673 --> 00:03:33.273
and trust.

163
00:03:33.273 --> 00:03:34.693
Go from builder to consultant.

164
00:03:34.693 --> 00:03:35.853
From executor to mentor.

165
00:03:35.853 --> 00:03:37.633
And from delivering to ensuring it works.

166
00:03:37.633 --> 00:03:39.213
That's the kind of AI professional who

167
00:03:39.213 --> 00:03:40.533
will earn a living in 2027.

